{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The optional Executive baseline distinguishes frame identities from actor identities. Activation binds an actor to a frame. Executive, Worker and Reviewer are finite evaluation frames, not actor identities or an organisational hierarchy.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1523,
          "quote":"Frames and actors are separate identities: activation binds an actor to\n a frame",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EXECUTIVE_BASELINE.md#EXECUTIVE_BASELINE%2F17.%20Actor%20binding%20and%20independence",
          "start_line":1515
        }
      ],
      "key":"baseline_actor_binding",
      "label":"Baseline actor binding"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Qualification applies frame qualification and method self-qualification; it introduces no additional protocol. A profile trial evaluates this profile and its declared composition with the exact REFERENCE_FRAME basis, without reproducing either as peer authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1550,
          "quote":"Qualification of this profile applies frame qualification and method\nself-qualification (EVALUATION §7); it creates no other qualification\nprotocol",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EXECUTIVE_BASELINE.md#EXECUTIVE_BASELINE%2F18.%20Profile%20qualification",
          "start_line":1542
        }
      ],
      "key":"profile_qualification",
      "label":"Profile qualification"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The Executive Baseline Adoption Additions template supplies profile-specific fields to the adoption record owned by §16. It starts from PRODUCT_DEFINITION §7's Project Reference-Frame Basis and starter template; it is neither a competing basis definition nor adoption authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1648,
          "quote":"This profile adds only the\nfollowing profile-specific content; it is not a competing basis definition\nor adoption authority",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EXECUTIVE_BASELINE.md#EXECUTIVE_BASELINE%2F19.%20Baseline-specific%20basis%20and%20activation%20additions%2FProject-basis%20additions",
          "start_line":1645
        }
      ],
      "key":"baseline_adoption_additions",
      "label":"Executive Baseline Adoption Additions"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The §16 adoption record is the referenced owner of the adoption list. The additions and that list form one record; overlapping items are decided by the §16 statement.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1642,
          "quote":"an adopting Product\nrecords both lists as one record, and where an item appears in both, the\n§16 statement decides it",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EXECUTIVE_BASELINE.md#EXECUTIVE_BASELINE%2F19.%20Baseline-specific%20basis%20and%20activation%20additions%2FProject-basis%20additions",
          "start_line":1640
        }
      ],
      "key":"referenced_adoption_record",
      "label":"Referenced §16 adoption record"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Profile-specific activation bindings supplement the generic minimal activation binding of REFERENCE_FRAME §11 separately for Worker, Reviewer and Executive.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1675,
          "quote":"Every activation first carries the generic binding of REFERENCE_FRAME §11\n(minimal activation binding). This profile adds, by frame",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EXECUTIVE_BASELINE.md#EXECUTIVE_BASELINE%2F19.%20Baseline-specific%20basis%20and%20activation%20additions%2FActivation%20additions",
          "start_line":1673
        }
      ],
      "key":"profile_activation_bindings",
      "label":"Profile activation additions"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The failure-classification table maps observations to first diagnoses and routes evaluation. Its entries do not predetermine verdicts.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1686,
          "quote":"The table routes evaluation; it does not predetermine a verdict",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EXECUTIVE_BASELINE.md#EXECUTIVE_BASELINE%2F20.%20Failure%20classification",
          "start_line":1685
        }
      ],
      "key":"failure_classification",
      "label":"Failure classification"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The thirty-two anti-patterns identify misapplication of the profile, covering frame identities, authority, results, evidence, testing, causal attribution, generic families and technical triage.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1721,
          "quote":"The profile is misapplied when",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EXECUTIVE_BASELINE.md#EXECUTIVE_BASELINE%2F21.%20Anti-patterns",
          "start_line":1721
        }
      ],
      "key":"profile_anti_patterns",
      "label":"Profile anti-patterns"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Baseline closure applies the referenced TICKETS §7 condition to the exact selected outcome and engagement branch. It is distinct from reviewer approval, a review event, and a closed but indeterminate result. The twenty-four listed conditions describe applying this baseline to one outcome.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1803,
          "quote":"a\nwork item closes by that condition, not by a reviewer's approval",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EXECUTIVE_BASELINE.md#EXECUTIVE_BASELINE%2F22.%20Closure",
          "start_line":1797
        }
      ],
      "key":"baseline_closure",
      "label":"Baseline closure"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The source-disposition register records claimed conservation, proposed supersession, non-applicability and new material relative to rc.2 STDO_REFERENCE_FRAME_BASELINE.md. Routes are provenance only; conserved claims are said to be restated at tenant loci, including duplicate listings in ASSURANCE or EVALUATION where appropriate.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1885,
          "quote":"Every conserved row is restated in tenant text at its locus; a route is\nprovenance only.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EXECUTIVE_BASELINE.md#EXECUTIVE_BASELINE%2F23.%20Source%20dispositions",
          "start_line":1883
        }
      ],
      "key":"baseline_source_dispositions",
      "label":"Baseline predecessor-claim register"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The register proposes changes to stale held results, work-result pairing and direction, absent seam results, diagnostic order, and the first failure-classification row. Each affected row is expressly proposed and pending the product owner's acceptance; synthetic child anchors identify split clauses.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1904,
          "quote":"superseded, proposed and pending the product owner's acceptance",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EXECUTIVE_BASELINE.md#EXECUTIVE_BASELINE%2F23.%20Source%20dispositions",
          "start_line":1904
        }
      ],
      "key":"pending_baseline_revisions",
      "label":"Pending baseline revisions"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"FOUNDATIONS is a Tier 1 member owning standing vocabulary; ontology and imports; commitments P0–P6, foundation-to-law format and material-family index; composition, conservation, core results, conjunction and categorical demonstration requirements; and hypotheses. Other members import or refine these definitions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":13,
          "quote":"Other members import these definitions\n([Imports between members](#imports-between-members)) or refine them.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS",
          "start_line":10
        }
      ],
      "key":"foundations_member",
      "label":"FOUNDATIONS member"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Foundational standing grounds a commitment or hypothesis relative to other foundational claims. Exactly four standings are defined: recovered, an adopted premise consolidating named source rules without treating them as proof; independent, an adopted premise argued by a case where other commitments hold and it fails; derived, a consequence naming its premises; hypothesis, a claim established by neither method clause nor argument, held for falsification and not law. This differs from result standing and a successor's disposition of a predecessor claim.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":31,
          "quote":"Each has exactly one of four standings:",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F1.%20Vocabulary%20of%20standing",
          "start_line":21
        }
      ],
      "key":"foundational_standing",
      "label":"Foundational standing"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Four distinct meanings of join are named: categorical join is a map between categories; composition boundary joins successive steps; join of results combines closed results under one decision rule; relation join is equality joining participants in an enclosing relation. An unqualified join in a result context means join of results.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":50,
          "quote":"Four uses of \"join\" in this corpus are distinct",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F1.%20Vocabulary%20of%20standing",
          "start_line":42
        }
      ],
      "key":"join_meanings",
      "label":"Four meanings of join"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A Subject is the exact thing a claim, representation or transformation concerns, with inventory and bytes or semantic addresses. A Representation is bytes, text, model or program standing for it; equal representations do not establish equal meaning. An Interpretation declares the representation-to-meaning contract within a Context, including reliable distinctions and residuals. Context declares subject, definitions, basis and admissible observations.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":59,
          "quote":"| Interpretation | a declared contract from representation to meaning within a context; says which distinctions a consumer may rely on and which are residual | P1 |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology",
          "start_line":58
        }
      ],
      "key":"subject_representation_interpretation",
      "label":"Subject, representation, interpretation and context"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Outcome U is a selected, owner-defined, directly verifiable state with an acceptance interval against which transformation adequacy is judged. Object O is a subject with scoped identity (authority, kind, value), boundary, current state and declared material relations; its identity survives admissible state and encoding changes.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":61,
          "quote":"| Outcome U | a selected, owner-defined, directly verifiable state with an acceptance interval; adequacy of a transformation is judged against it | P2 |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology",
          "start_line":60
        }
      ],
      "key":"outcome_and_object",
      "label":"Outcome and object"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"An Actor performs a step within a declared context, attention, time and capability envelope. A role is a relation the actor occupies, with available roles determined by configuration. A Step is bounded work comprising intake, selected context, grant, execution and output. A Step-produced fact is a judgment, residual, observation or authority decision that cannot be reacquired from declared sources; reproducible observations are excluded.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":64,
          "quote":"| Step-produced fact | a judgment, residual, observation or authority decision produced by a step that cannot be re-acquired from declared sources; an observation reproducible from declared sources is re-acquirable and is not one | P0, P4b |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology",
          "start_line":64
        }
      ],
      "key":"actor_step_and_fact",
      "label":"Actor, step and step-produced fact"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A Claim asserts a property of a subject with basis, witness relation and falsifier. Evidence consists of exact admitted observations or artifacts, each carrying provenance, subject identity, basis and scope; availability in a frame supplies neither semantic truth nor decision authority. A Judgment records evaluation of an unchanged subject, evaluator, decision, evidence, scope and revising observation. Authority is identity-bearing permission to operate or dispose acceptance within scope. Residual is material uncertainty, omission, loss or unproven relation retained until authorised disposition.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":69,
          "quote":"| Judgment | a recorded evaluation over an unchanged subject, with evaluator, decision, evidence, scope and revising observation | P6 |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology",
          "start_line":67
        }
      ],
      "key":"claim_evidence_judgment_authority_residual",
      "label":"Claim, evidence, judgment, authority and residual"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Basis is the exact state bound by a step, claim, result or evaluation, including every version, identity, time, topology, authority, configuration or evidence coordinate whose change can alter it. It is immutable or moving with declared advancement detection and reevaluation conditions; mixed bases require an explicit composite relation. Materiality is evaluation-relative: lawful change can change the result, invalidate basis, change admissible evidence, change who may perform a material operation, or change who may decide the evaluation or a material operation. The same test applies to uncertainty or closure; materiality in one frame need not hold in another.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":71,
          "quote":"mixed bases need an explicit composite relation",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology",
          "start_line":70
        }
      ],
      "key":"basis_and_materiality",
      "label":"Basis and materiality"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Evaluation is a declared question with known admissible result family, evidence basis and decision authority, plus operation authority where it performs material action. Questions may concern judgment, prediction, construction, comparison, proof, refusal, classification or action recommendation. An Evaluation family requires the same material-relation manifold, authority basis and result algebra; similar wording does not establish family identity.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":113,
          "quote":"similar wording does not prove that two evaluations share a family",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology%2FFrame%20objects",
          "start_line":113
        }
      ],
      "key":"evaluation_and_family",
      "label":"Evaluation and evaluation family"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Workspace is the bounded material reasoned over or acted upon, potentially including constitutional sources, assets, designs, implementations, tools, runtime state, evidence, history, external dependencies and human decisions; a repository is one possible carrier. A Material-relation manifold is the finite, jointly interpretable variables, entities, identities, states, relations, invariants, authorities, observations and exclusions needed by an evaluation family. Its charts may overlap; no geometric mathematics or runtime abstraction is implied.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":115,
          "quote":"no geometric mathematics or runtime abstraction is implied",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology%2FFrame%20objects",
          "start_line":115
        }
      ],
      "key":"workspace_and_manifold",
      "label":"Workspace and material-relation manifold"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Coordinate is the complete local position for interpreting a value, event, decision, observation or action, including required basis, identity, lineage, input, evidence and authority. Equality follows a declared relation, not equal names, values, shapes, locations or apparent topology positions. Topology is the material entities, capabilities, authorities, lifecycle state spaces, adjacencies and seams visible to a frame at one basis; it need not be the whole workspace topology. State-value change preserves topology only if every such constituent is preserved; changing any is a topology mutation that may invalidate coordinates.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":117,
          "quote":"a frame need not hold the workspace's complete topology",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology%2FFrame%20objects",
          "start_line":117
        }
      ],
      "key":"coordinate_and_topology",
      "label":"Coordinate and topology"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Capability envelope is the competence, context capacity, tools, access, applicable evaluation or assurance independence conditions, and configuration needed to activate and evaluate a frame. The same frame can be usable by one actor and unusable by another. Capability can justify different frame size or activation sequence, but cannot waive invariants or widen authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":118,
          "quote":"actor capability may justify a different frame size or activation sequence and can neither waive an invariant nor widen authority",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology%2FFrame%20objects",
          "start_line":118
        }
      ],
      "key":"capability_envelope",
      "label":"Capability envelope"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Reference frame is a finite specification of attention, coordinates, authority, evidence and result relations for one declared evaluation family over a stated basis. Activated frame binds one declaration to exact evaluation, workspace subject, basis, actor and evidence-acquisition boundary; declaration reuse does not prove activation correctness. These objects are defined in FOUNDATIONS, with laws assigned to REFERENCE_FRAME and activating steps to ACTIVATION.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":120,
          "quote":"a reusable declaration is not evidence that a particular activation was correct",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology%2FFrame%20objects",
          "start_line":120
        }
      ],
      "key":"reference_and_activated_frame",
      "label":"Reference frame and activated frame"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Frame set is a human-declared family available for a governed outcome or workspace. Active frame configuration is the activations and declared result relations used for one evaluation or decision, potentially one frame or several disjoint, overlapping or composed frames. Lens is a reusable question family; abstract authority, capability and reuse, temporal behaviour, transport and proof may be lenses. A lens becomes a frame only with bounded evaluation, basis, manifold, evidence, authorities, result algebra and capability envelope. Role allocates responsibility or admitted authority; persona changes approach or salience; abstraction hides or contracts detail; component is a structural part. None is a reference frame by identity alone.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":124,
          "quote":"a lens becomes a frame only when instantiated with a bounded evaluation, basis, material manifold, evidence, authorities, result algebra and capability envelope",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology%2FFrame%20objects",
          "start_line":123
        }
      ],
      "key":"frame_configuration_and_related_roles",
      "label":"Frame sets, configurations, lenses and related roles"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Authority kinds are frame-set authority (human declaration or acceptance of a project frame set), semantic authority (meaning owner), evaluation authority (role permitted to evaluate), operation authority (owner-issued grant to construct, transform, admit, publish or mutate a material subject), and decision authority (role permitted to accept, reject or otherwise dispose a result). Actor identity names the performer and is not authority. Kinds may coincide lawfully but never collapse by default.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":139,
          "quote":"These may coincide in a lawful case and\nnever collapse by default.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology%2FAuthority%20kinds",
          "start_line":136
        }
      ],
      "key":"authority_kinds",
      "label":"Authority kinds"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Frame disjointness is structural absence of shared material variables, coordinates, evidence or result dependencies for declared evaluations. Evaluation independence is procedural separation: separately activated evaluations neither consume nor influence one another's unclosed working results, though subjects and manifolds may overlap and closed results may later enter one conjunction. Assurance independence is claim-relative, determined by governing assurance law's non-authorship, acquisition, exposure, actor and configuration separation conditions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":204,
          "quote":"Three relations stay distinct",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology%2FDisjointness%20and%20independence",
          "start_line":192
        }
      ],
      "key":"disjointness_and_independence",
      "label":"Three distinct separation relations"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The frame tuple is ⟨Q, B, M, C, I, A, E, X, R, J, K, D⟩: evaluation family; workspace/topology basis; manifold; coordinate/equality law; applicable invariants; authority relation; admissible evidence/provenance law; exclusions/prohibited assumptions; closed result algebra or core-mapped refinement; invalidation/rechart condition; capability envelope; optional declared dependencies, overlaps, inputs and outputs involving other frames. It specifies an evaluation contract, not a mandatory schema or runtime carrier.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":218,
          "quote":"The tuple specifies the relation; it is not a required schema or runtime\ncarrier",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology%2FThe%20frame%20tuple",
          "start_line":217
        }
      ],
      "key":"frame_tuple",
      "label":"Frame tuple"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The recursive-product taxonomy, owned here under urn:stdo:bounded-context:recursive-product-taxonomy, retains concept identities under urn:stdo:concept:recursive-product-taxonomy:. Substrate is a lower product/runtime for building products. Development product is the role of one exact released-product install as builder substrate for a source project; it creates no new product identity or transfer of meaning or authority. Source project is the mutable workspace building the next release cut. Artifact is any published output built by a source project or configured installed product.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":248,
          "quote":"the role creates no new product identity and transfers no meaning or authority from the builder to the product being authored",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology%2FProduct%20lineage%20objects",
          "start_line":243
        }
      ],
      "key":"recursive_product_roles",
      "label":"Substrate, development product, source project and artifact"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Release cut is one immutable annotated release-candidate boundary over a published feature set. Product is the released immutable thing resulting when that cut is accepted. Install is a stamped workspace instance of that Product. Product definition is the present-tense source-project authority surface, conventionally PRODUCT.md, defining current WHAT for decomposition; it is not the released artifact. Their carried concept identities use the recursive-product-taxonomy namespace with release-cut, product, install and product-definition suffixes.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":249,
          "quote":"| Product (`urn:stdo:concept:recursive-product-taxonomy:product`) | the released immutable thing resulting when that release cut is accepted | P3 |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology%2FProduct%20lineage%20objects",
          "start_line":246
        }
      ],
      "key":"release_product_install_definition",
      "label":"Release cut, Product, install and Product definition"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
